{"cefr": "B2", "topic": "science", "body": "Coral reefs occupy less than one per cent of the ocean floor but shelter roughly a quarter of all marine species. The structure that makes this possible is built by animals barely a few millimetres across, which extract calcium carbonate from seawater and deposit it beneath themselves. Each generation builds on the skeletons of the last, so a reef is mostly the remains of its own history. What allows corals to grow at that rate is a partnership. Algae live inside the coral's tissue, using sunlight to produce sugars that the coral consumes, and in return receive shelter and a steady supply of waste chemicals they can use. Neither organism does well alone. When water stays unusually warm, the partnership breaks down. The coral expels the algae, loses its colour, and is left living on stored reserves. If conditions return to normal quickly the algae can be reacquired. If they do not, the coral starves. What worries researchers is not any single warm season but the shortening gap between them, which leaves less time for recovery than a reef needs.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is mainly concerned with", "correct_answer": "how reefs are built and why a warming ocean threatens the relationship that sustains them", "distractors": ["the number of marine species that depend on coral for food", "the chemical process by which calcium carbonate forms in seawater", "methods researchers use to measure ocean temperature"], "explanation": "The passage explains reef construction, then the algal partnership, then what heat does to it. Species counts and calcium carbonate are single details, and no measurement method is described.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, the algae living inside coral tissue", "correct_answer": "produce sugars that the coral consumes", "distractors": ["build the calcium carbonate skeleton", "protect the coral from unusually warm water", "are expelled every year as part of normal growth"], "explanation": "The passage states the algae use sunlight to make sugars the coral eats. The skeleton is built by the coral itself, and expulsion is described as a breakdown rather than a normal cycle.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that a bleached coral can survive if", "correct_answer": "normal conditions return before its stored reserves run out", "distractors": ["it relocates to deeper and cooler water", "it produces a new skeleton without the algae", "the water remains warm long enough for new algae to adapt"], "explanation": "The coral lives on stored reserves and can reacquire algae if conditions normalise quickly, which sets survival against how long the reserves last. Corals do not relocate, and prolonged warmth is given as the cause of starvation, not a remedy.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"partnership\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "an arrangement in which both organisms benefit", "distractors": ["a legal business agreement", "a temporary alliance against a threat", "a competition for the same resources"], "explanation": "The paragraph describes each organism supplying what the other needs and states that neither does well alone. The word is biological here, not legal, and the relationship is cooperative rather than competitive.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author notes that a reef is mostly the remains of its own history in order to", "correct_answer": "convey that the structure accumulates slowly over many generations", "distractors": ["suggest that most reefs are already dead", "explain why reefs are found in shallow water", "argue that reefs cannot recover once damaged"], "explanation": "The sentence follows the description of each generation building on the skeletons of the last, making the point about accumulated time. The passage explicitly allows recovery when warm periods are short.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
{"cefr": "C1", "topic": "economics", "body": "Economists have long puzzled over why identical goods sell for different prices in the same city. Standard theory predicts that shoppers will find the cheaper seller and the difference will disappear. In practice it persists, sometimes for years. Part of the answer is that searching costs something. Comparing prices takes time, and for an inexpensive item the effort can exceed the saving. Sellers know this, and a shop in a convenient location can charge more without losing custom, because the cost of walking further is real even when it is not counted in money. A second factor is that shoppers differ. Some will search extensively and some will not, and a market containing both can support two prices indefinitely: the low price captures the searchers, the high price captures everyone else. Neither seller has any reason to move. This has an awkward implication. Making price information easier to obtain does not simply help consumers, because sellers observe the same change and may respond by matching each other rather than competing. Several studies of online markets have found prices converging upward after comparison tools appeared.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is primarily concerned with", "correct_answer": "why identical goods can hold different prices, and why better information may not fix it", "distractors": ["the history of price regulation in urban markets", "how online retailers calculate their delivery costs", "the difficulty of measuring inflation accurately"], "explanation": "The passage explains persistent price differences through search costs and differing shoppers, then turns to the unexpected effect of comparison tools. Regulation, delivery and inflation appear nowhere.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, a conveniently located shop can charge more because", "correct_answer": "the effort of going elsewhere is a genuine cost to the shopper", "distractors": ["its rent is higher than that of shops further away", "its customers are wealthier on average", "it stocks goods that competitors cannot obtain"], "explanation": "The passage says the cost of walking further is real even when not counted in money. Rent, wealth and exclusive stock are never mentioned.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that two prices can persist because", "correct_answer": "each price is profitable against a different kind of shopper", "distractors": ["sellers have agreed privately not to undercut each other", "the cheaper seller cannot supply the whole market", "shoppers are unable to tell the goods apart"], "explanation": "The low price captures searchers and the high price captures everyone else, so neither seller gains by moving. The passage describes this as an outcome of shopper differences, not an agreement or a supply limit.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"custom\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "the trade of regular customers", "distractors": ["a traditional practice", "an import duty", "a personal habit"], "explanation": "The phrase is 'charge more without losing custom', which concerns business a shop retains. The word's other senses — tradition, duty, habit — make no sense of what a shop stands to lose by raising prices.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author mentions studies of online markets primarily to", "correct_answer": "give evidence for the counterintuitive claim just made", "distractors": ["argue that online shopping is more expensive than shopping in person", "explain how comparison tools are built", "recommend that comparison tools be regulated"], "explanation": "The studies appear immediately after the claim that better information may cause sellers to match rather than compete, and report prices converging upward — evidence for that claim. The passage makes no recommendation.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
{"cefr": "B2", "topic": "history", "body": "The medieval guilds of northern Europe are often remembered as associations of craftsmen, but their most important function was controlling entry. A guild decided who could practise a trade in a town, how long an apprenticeship lasted, and what counted as acceptable work. Anyone producing goods outside that structure risked having them seized. This has attracted opposite judgements. Critics point out that guilds restricted competition and kept prices artificially high, and that the length of an apprenticeship often had more to do with securing cheap labour than with any real need for training. Defenders reply that in an age without brands or inspectors, a guild mark was the only assurance a buyer had, and that standards would otherwise have collapsed. Both readings can be supported from the records, which is part of why the argument has lasted. What is clearer is why guilds declined. Production began moving out to villages beyond a town's authority, where the rules simply did not reach and labour could be had more cheaply. By the time governments began abolishing guilds outright, much of the work they governed had already left.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is mainly about", "correct_answer": "what guilds actually controlled, the disagreement about their effect, and how they lost their hold", "distractors": ["the techniques used by medieval craftsmen", "the laws that governments passed to abolish guilds", "the training that apprentices received in northern Europe"], "explanation": "The passage sets out the guilds' control of entry, presents both judgements of it, and closes on their decline. Abolition gets one clause and is explicitly described as arriving late.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, someone producing goods outside the guild structure", "correct_answer": "risked having those goods confiscated", "distractors": ["was required to pay a fee to the town", "could sell them only in nearby villages", "had to complete a longer apprenticeship"], "explanation": "The passage states such goods risked being seized. Villages appear much later, as where production moved, and the apprenticeship point belongs to the criticism of guilds.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that abolishing the guilds", "correct_answer": "confirmed a decline that had already largely happened", "distractors": ["caused production to move out of the towns", "was resisted successfully for several decades", "restored competition to the urban trades"], "explanation": "Much of the work had already left by the time abolition came, so the laws followed the change rather than causing it. The passage gives the movement to villages as the cause.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"assurance\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "a guarantee of quality", "distractors": ["a confident manner", "an insurance policy", "a formal promise of payment"], "explanation": "The guild mark is described as what a buyer had in an age without brands or inspectors, so it stands for evidence that goods were sound. Confidence, insurance and payment do not fit that role.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author states that both readings can be supported from the records in order to", "correct_answer": "explain why the disagreement has proved so durable", "distractors": ["suggest that the surviving records are unreliable", "show that the critics have the stronger case", "argue that the question is not worth pursuing"], "explanation": "The clause is followed directly by 'which is part of why the argument has lasted'. It accounts for the persistence of the dispute rather than casting doubt on the evidence or picking a side.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
{"cefr": "B2", "topic": "psychology", "body": "People are markedly worse at predicting how they will feel about a future event than they expect to be. Asked how a promotion, a move or a rejection will affect them, most overestimate both the strength of the reaction and how long it will last. The effect has been demonstrated with events as different as sporting defeats, examination results and the end of relationships. One explanation is that when we imagine a future situation, we picture it in isolation. The mind supplies the event and little else, while real life supplies a great deal else: the meal that follows, the work still waiting, the ordinary irritations that continue regardless. Those competing demands dilute the feeling long before the imagined version would have faded. A second explanation is that people underestimate their own capacity to make sense of what happens. Explanations are found, disappointments are reinterpreted, and the event settles into a story that is easier to carry. The process is largely invisible from the outside, and largely invisible in advance, which is why it is so consistently left out of the forecast.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is primarily concerned with", "correct_answer": "why people misjudge the strength and duration of their future feelings", "distractors": ["the emotional effects of losing a job", "how researchers measure happiness over time", "the differences between imagined and remembered events"], "explanation": "The passage establishes the misprediction, then gives two explanations for it. Specific events are examples, and no measurement method is described.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, when people imagine a future event they tend to", "correct_answer": "picture it without the ordinary demands that would surround it", "distractors": ["recall a similar event from their own past", "focus on how others would react to it", "underestimate how likely it is to happen"], "explanation": "The passage says the mind supplies the event and little else, while real life supplies the meal, the work and the ordinary irritations. Memory, other people and likelihood are not discussed.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that the second explanation is easy to overlook because", "correct_answer": "the process it describes is not noticeable while it is happening", "distractors": ["it applies only to negative events", "it has less evidence supporting it than the first", "it contradicts what the first explanation claims"], "explanation": "The passage calls the process largely invisible from the outside and in advance, which is why it is left out of forecasts. It is not presented as weaker than or opposed to the first explanation.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"dilute\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "weaken by mixing with other things", "distractors": ["postpone until later", "explain away entirely", "make more difficult to describe"], "explanation": "The competing demands of real life reduce the feeling's intensity, which is what diluting does. The sentence says the feeling fades sooner, not that it is delayed or explained.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author lists a meal, waiting work and ordinary irritations in order to", "correct_answer": "make concrete what the imagined version of an event leaves out", "distractors": ["show that daily life is more stressful than people admit", "suggest that these things cause the emotion to intensify", "illustrate how researchers design their experiments"], "explanation": "The list follows 'real life supplies a great deal else' and gives examples of that else. Those demands are said to dilute the feeling rather than intensify it.", "difficulty_est": 4}]}
{"cefr": "C1", "topic": "society", "body": "Almost every country measures unemployment by asking whether people are working, and whether those who are not have looked for work recently. The definition sounds straightforward until it is applied. Someone who worked one paid hour in the reference week counts as employed. Someone who wants a job but stopped searching after a year of refusals does not count as unemployed, because they are classified as outside the labour force altogether. The result is a figure that can move for reasons unconnected to how many people have work. A downturn severe enough to discourage searching will push the unemployment rate down, since the discouraged leave the count entirely. Recoveries can do the opposite: as prospects improve, people resume looking, are counted again, and the rate rises even as conditions get better. Statistical agencies are well aware of this and routinely publish several broader measures alongside the headline figure. Those measures are almost never the ones quoted in a news report. The narrow rate has the advantage of being comparable across decades and countries, and a number that everyone already uses is difficult to displace, whatever its shortcomings.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is mainly concerned with", "correct_answer": "how the standard definition of unemployment can mislead, and why it persists anyway", "distractors": ["the causes of long-term unemployment in wealthy countries", "a comparison of unemployment between different countries", "the effect of downturns on the number of hours people work"], "explanation": "The passage sets out the definition, shows how it moves for unrelated reasons, and ends on why it survives. Causes of unemployment and cross-country comparison are not the subject.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, someone who has given up looking after a year of refusals is counted as", "correct_answer": "outside the labour force rather than unemployed", "distractors": ["unemployed but not actively seeking", "employed if they worked at any point that year", "excluded from all official statistics"], "explanation": "The passage states they are classified as outside the labour force altogether. They do appear in the broader measures the passage mentions, so they are not excluded from all statistics.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that a rising unemployment rate during a recovery", "correct_answer": "may reflect people returning to the search rather than conditions worsening", "distractors": ["indicates that the recovery has already stalled", "results from employers reporting their figures late", "shows that the broader measures are inaccurate"], "explanation": "As prospects improve people resume looking and are counted again, so the rate rises even as conditions get better. Nothing suggests late reporting or a fault in the broader measures.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the last sentence, \"displace\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "replace with something else", "distractors": ["move to another country", "calculate a second time", "remove from public view"], "explanation": "The sentence concerns a widely used number being hard to replace with a better one, despite its shortcomings. Relocation, recalculation and concealment do not fit what happens to a statistic in use.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author mentions that broader measures are published but rarely quoted in order to", "correct_answer": "show that the problem is not one of missing information but of which number gets used", "distractors": ["criticise statistical agencies for hiding the fuller picture", "argue that the broader measures are too complicated", "suggest that journalists misunderstand the figures"], "explanation": "The agencies already publish the fuller measures, so the gap is in what gets quoted rather than in what exists. The final sentence attributes that to comparability and habit rather than to concealment or complexity.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
